/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Society: law, power and government */
WB.L("code-of-hammurabi", "society", "beginner", "Hammurabi's Code: law carved in stone",
  "Nearly 4,000 years ago a Babylonian king had his laws carved into a towering black stone for everyone to see. It is one of the oldest legal texts we can still read, and it is far stranger and more human than 'an eye for an eye' suggests.",
  [
    ["A stone in the sand", "The best-known copy of Hammurabi's laws was found in Iran in 1901, far from Babylon, where it was made.",
      "In the winter of 1901 to 1902, French archaeologists at Susa, in what is now south-western Iran, unearthed three pieces of polished black stone. Fitted together, they formed a pillar over two metres tall, covered in tiny wedge-shaped cuneiform writing in the Akkadian language. It was a law code issued by Hammurabi, king of Babylon, who ruled from about 1792 to 1750 BCE. But Susa is not in Babylon. Scholars think an Elamite king carried the stone off as war plunder in the 12th century BCE. It now stands in the Louvre in Paris.\n\nHammurabi began as ruler of one city among several competing ones in Mesopotamia. Over about four decades, diplomacy, alliances and war brought much of the south and centre under his control."],
    ["Justice from the sun god", "The stone presents the king's laws as justice backed by the gods, meant to protect the weak from the strong.",
      "Running a large kingdom meant managing canals, taxes and disputes between people with different local customs. Letters from Hammurabi's reign show a hands-on ruler, writing to officials about everything from flood repairs to a single stolen field. His law collection, probably set up late in his reign, was meant to show that he had brought order and fairness.\n\nAt the top of the stone, a carving shows Hammurabi standing before a seated god, usually identified as Shamash, the sun god and god of justice. A prologue lists the king's achievements, then come the laws, then an epilogue. In it he says he set up the laws so the strong would not oppress the weak, and invites any wronged person to have the stone read to them."],
    ["Laws for everyday life", "The roughly 282 laws cover ordinary life, from farming and trade to marriage and medicine.",
      "Scholars count about 282 laws, though a section near the end was deliberately rubbed away in antiquity and has been partly recovered from other copies. Most follow a simple pattern: if someone does this, then this happens.\n\nThe subjects are surprisingly ordinary. There are rules on renting fields and boats, interest on loans, wages for workers, fees for doctors, inheritance, divorce and the care of children. A builder whose badly built house collapsed and killed its owner could be put to death. A tavern keeper who cheated customers over beer could be punished. Reading the laws is like looking through a window at the worries of ordinary Babylonians: debts, floods, unpaid bills and family quarrels."],
    ["Eye for an eye", "Punishments depended heavily on the social rank of both victim and offender.",
      "The code's most famous principle is retaliation: if a man destroys the eye of another man, his own eye will be destroyed. But this applied mainly between people of the same high status. Babylonian society had roughly three ranks: free citizens, a middle group of commoners or dependants, and enslaved people.\n\nIf a free man blinded a commoner, he paid a fine in silver instead. If he injured an enslaved person, he paid compensation to the owner. Women's rights depended on their family position, and some laws protected wives and widows. The code is both fair-minded and deeply unequal, a reminder that every legal system reflects the values of its time."],
    ["Not first, not rulebook", "Older law codes existed, historians debate how the code was used, and its lasting idea is public, written law.",
      "Hammurabi is often called the first lawgiver, but older collections survive, including one linked to Ur-Nammu of Ur, roughly three centuries earlier. His stone stands out because it is so complete.\n\nHistorians also debate its purpose. Thousands of Babylonian contracts and court records survive, yet they rarely cite the code. Many scholars think it was less a statute book than a royal showcase of wise judgments for judges and scribes to learn from. Scribes copied it for centuries.\n\nIts big ideas still matter: laws should be written down and public, punishment should be proportionate, judges should not change decisions once made, and false accusers should be punished. They resurface from Roman law to modern Australian courts."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why was the best-known copy of the code found in Iran rather than Babylon?", ["Hammurabi ordered it carved at Susa","Babylonian merchants traded it to Susa","Scholars think an Elamite king took it as war plunder","French archaeologists moved it there in 1901"], 2, "It was made in Babylon and carried to Susa centuries later as plunder."],
    [1, "What did Hammurabi say his laws were meant to achieve?", ["That the strong would not oppress the weak","That every citizen would pay equal taxes","That foreign kingdoms would pay tribute","That priests would hold all legal power"], 0, "The epilogue presents the laws as protection for the weak, backed by the sun god of justice."],
    [2, "What kind of matters do most of the laws cover?", ["Rituals for temples and festivals","Military strategy and battle plans","Ceremonies for the royal court","Daily life, such as trade, wages and debts"], 3, "The laws are full of ordinary concerns such as loans, fees, inheritance and building."],
    [3, "What usually happened if a free man blinded a commoner?", ["He lost his own eye","He paid a fine in silver","He was banished from Babylon","He faced no consequence"], 1, "Exact retaliation applied mainly between equals, while injuring a lower-ranked person usually meant a payment."],
    [4, "Why do many historians doubt the code worked like a modern statute book?", ["It was destroyed soon after it was made","It contained only a small number of laws","Contracts and court records rarely cite it","It was hidden away from judges and scribes"], 2, "Babylonian legal documents seldom refer to it, so it may have been a model of royal justice instead."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Why the same injury could cost a fine or an eye in ancient Babylon."});

WB.L("trial-by-jury", "society", "beginner", "Trial by jury: twelve ordinary people",
  "Somewhere in Australia today, twelve strangers are sitting in a small room deciding whether someone is guilty of a serious crime. Why do we hand such power to ordinary citizens, and what actually happens if your name is drawn?",
  [
    ["Called by chance", "Jurors are drawn at random from the electoral roll, and answering a summons is a legal duty.",
      "An envelope from the sheriff's office or court tells you that you have been selected for possible jury service. It is a normal part of being enrolled to vote: in every state and territory, potential jurors are chosen at random from the electoral roll. Not everyone summoned serves. Some people are ineligible, such as many lawyers, police officers and politicians, and others can be excused for good reasons, like caring duties or serious hardship.\n\nRespond by the deadline, because ignoring a summons can lead to a fine. Employers generally cannot penalise you for serving, and jurors receive an allowance, though amounts and wage top-ups vary by state. This is general information, not legal advice, so check your state or territory court's juror pages."],
    ["From ordeals to juries", "Juries replaced trial by ordeal in England, and a 1670 case made jurors free from punishment for their verdicts.",
      "In early medieval England, guilt could be tested by ordeal: carrying a red-hot iron or being lowered into water, with the outcome believed to reveal God's judgment. In 1215 the Fourth Lateran Council banned priests from taking part, which made ordeals unworkable. Courts turned to groups of local men already asked about crimes in their area, who gradually became neutral judges of evidence.\n\nIn 1670 the jury trying Quakers William Penn and William Mead refused to convict, even after the judge locked the jurors up and fined them. Juror Edward Bushel challenged his imprisonment. In Bushel's Case, Sir John Vaughan ruled that jurors cannot be punished for a verdict the judge dislikes. The principle travelled with English law to Australia."],
    ["Juries in Australia", "Juries mainly hear serious criminal trials, usually with twelve members.",
      "Most legal matters in Australia do not involve a jury. Minor offences and most civil disputes, such as contract claims, are decided by a magistrate or judge alone. Juries mainly decide serious criminal cases, such as murder, sexual offences, serious assault and large-scale fraud, and criminal juries usually have twelve members.\n\nSection 80 of the Constitution says a trial on indictment for a Commonwealth offence must be by jury, though the High Court has read that protection fairly narrowly. In some states an accused person can choose a judge-alone trial in certain circumstances. Civil juries are rare, but some states still allow them in particular cases, such as some defamation claims."],
    ["Inside the jury room", "Jurors decide the facts from the evidence presented in court, while the judge explains the law.",
      "The judge controls the courtroom, rules on evidence and explains the law. The jury decides what actually happened. In a criminal case, jurors must be satisfied of guilt beyond reasonable doubt, a high standard designed to protect innocent people from wrongful conviction. They must decide only on evidence presented in court. Doing your own research, searching online about the case or visiting the scene can be a criminal offence, because it can lead to an unfair trial. Jury room discussions stay confidential even after the case ends. Verdicts traditionally had to be unanimous, but most states now allow majority verdicts in some circumstances after long deliberation. Some trials involve distressing evidence, and courts in most states offer free, confidential counselling to jurors afterwards."],
    ["The case for and against", "Supporters value juries as a democratic check on the state, while critics question their consistency and cost.",
      "Supporters say juries bring community values into the courtroom and check the power of the state, since a government cannot imprison someone for a serious crime without convincing ordinary citizens. Juries also spread responsibility for grave decisions across many people and give citizens a direct role in justice. Many jurors find the experience absorbing and meaningful.\n\nCritics raise real concerns. Complex fraud or scientific evidence can be hard for non-experts to follow, jury trials take longer and cost more, and jurors may be swayed by media coverage or personal impressions. Some countries, such as Japan, use mixed panels of judges and citizens instead. Most Australian legal experts accept the system is imperfect, and debate about how to improve it continues."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Where do Australian courts draw potential jurors from?", ["Tax office records","The electoral roll","Driver licence databases","Online volunteers"], 1, "Jurors are chosen at random from people enrolled to vote."],
    [1, "What did Bushel's Case in 1670 establish?", ["Jury verdicts must always be unanimous","Women were allowed to serve on juries","Jurors cannot be punished for a verdict","Judges could overrule any jury verdict"], 2, "The ruling made a jury's verdict its own, free from official pressure."],
    [2, "Which cases do juries mainly decide in Australia?", ["Serious criminal cases such as murder","Most civil disputes, such as contract claims","Minor offences such as parking fines","Appeals heard in the High Court of Australia"], 0, "Minor offences and most civil matters go to a magistrate or judge alone."],
    [3, "Why must jurors avoid researching a case online?", ["Court websites block most such searches","It is allowed but strongly frowned upon","It only matters in some civil trials","It can be an offence and derail the trial"], 3, "Jurors must decide only on the evidence presented in court."],
    [4, "What is one common criticism of jury trials?", ["Juries always favour the prosecution","Complex evidence is hard for non-experts","Juries are hand-picked by politicians","Jurors never get to hear any evidence"], 1, "Critics worry about complex cases, cost, delays and outside influence."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Ordinary people can decide a murder trial. How that works, and why."});

WB.L("separation-of-powers", "society", "beginner", "Separation of powers: why no one holds all the keys",
  "Who makes the laws, who carries them out, and who decides what they mean? Keeping those three jobs apart is one of the oldest safeguards against tyranny, and Australia does it in its own distinctive way.",
  [
    ["Three keys to one vault", "Government power splits into three jobs, and keeping them apart makes abuse of power harder.",
      "Imagine a vault that needs three keys turned at once, held by three different people. No one can empty it alone. That is the idea behind the separation of powers. The legislature makes laws; in Australia, that is Parliament. The executive runs the country day to day, including the Prime Minister, ministers and the public service. The judiciary interprets laws and settles disputes: the courts. If one group controlled all three, it could write any rule, enforce it however it liked and judge its own case.\n\nThe French thinker Montesquieu made the case in The Spirit of the Laws (1748): liberty is at risk when one body both makes and enforces laws, or when judges are not separate from rulers. His reading of England was imperfect, but his argument shaped modern democracies."],
    ["The American model", "The United States separates its three branches strictly and builds in checks and balances.",
      "The framers of the United States Constitution in 1787 took Montesquieu seriously. They placed Congress, the President and the Supreme Court in separate articles and kept their members apart: the President does not sit in Congress, and members of Congress cannot serve in the cabinet. They added checks and balances so each branch can restrain the others. The President can veto bills, Congress can override a veto with a large enough majority, the Senate must approve many appointments, and the courts can strike down laws that breach the Constitution.\n\nSupporters say this makes power hard to abuse. Critics say it can make government slow and prone to deadlock when rival parties control different branches."],
    ["Australia's blend", "Australia's Constitution separates the courts firmly but deliberately fuses Parliament and the executive.",
      "Australia's founders borrowed from two traditions. From the United States came a written Constitution with separate chapters: Chapter I covers Parliament, Chapter II the executive and Chapter III the courts. From Britain came the Westminster system of responsible government, where ministers must sit in Parliament and answer to it. Section 64 says a minister must become a senator or member of the House within three months. So Parliament and the executive overlap heavily: the government is formed by whoever holds the confidence of the House of Representatives.\n\nWhat keeps this in check is that ministers can be questioned in Parliament every sitting day, can lose the House's confidence, and must face voters at regular elections."],
    ["Keeping judges apart", "The High Court has insisted that federal judicial power stays with real courts, and judges are protected from removal.",
      "Where Australia is strict is the judiciary. In the landmark 1956 Boilermakers' Case, the High Court ruled that a federal body could not combine judicial power, such as deciding legal guilt and punishment, with non-judicial functions like setting wages through arbitration. Judicial power under Chapter III must be exercised by courts, and courts should not be handed tasks that compromise their independence. Later cases extended some protection to state courts too, because they can exercise federal power.\n\nJudges are also protected in other ways. Federal judges can be removed only by the Governor-General after both houses of Parliament ask, on grounds of proved misbehaviour or incapacity, and their pay cannot be cut while they hold office."],
    ["Checks in everyday politics", "Many familiar parts of Australian politics are checks on power, and every democracy strikes its own balance.",
      "Once you know what to look for, checks appear everywhere. The Senate rejecting or amending a bill is one house checking another. Senate estimates hearings, where officials are grilled on spending, are Parliament checking the executive. A court ruling a government decision unlawful is the judiciary checking the executive. Auditors-General, ombudsmen and anti-corruption commissions add more, and a free press and the right to vote act as checks from outside government.\n\nThere is no perfect division. Separate power too strictly and government can grind to a halt. Concentrate it and those in charge may escape accountability. Britain relies heavily on convention, the United States has rigid separation, and Australia sits between. People tend to value checks more when their side is out of power."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does splitting government into three branches protect against abuse?", ["It guarantees that decisions are made quickly","No one group can make, enforce and judge the rules","It ensures all branches are run by the same party","It removes the need for regular elections"], 1, "If one group held all three powers, it could write, enforce and judge its own rules."],
    [1, "What is a common criticism of the strict American separation of powers?", ["Courts are barred from reviewing any laws","The President also sits in Congress","Congress is unable to pass any bills","Rival parties can deadlock the government"], 3, "Strict separation and checks make power hard to abuse, but can also stall decisions."],
    [2, "How does Australia differ from the United States on Parliament and the executive?", ["Ministers must sit in Parliament, so the two overlap","The Prime Minister is barred from entering Parliament","Ministers are appointed by the High Court judges","Ministers cannot be questioned in Parliament at all"], 0, "Australia's Westminster system fuses the two, and ministers answer to Parliament."],
    [3, "What did the High Court decide in the 1956 Boilermakers' Case?", ["Federal judges must be elected by the public","Parliament may overrule any court decision","Courts alone can exercise federal judicial power","States are not allowed to have their own courts"], 2, "The case set a strict separation of federal judicial power from other functions."],
    [4, "Which is an example of Parliament checking the executive?", ["A court hearing a criminal trial","Senate estimates hearings about spending","A newspaper publishing an editorial","A citizen lodging a tax return"], 1, "Estimates hearings let senators scrutinise how government departments spend money."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Why Australia's government is deliberately tangled, except for its courts."});

WB.L("the-dismissal-1975", "society", "intermediate", "The Dismissal: Australia's 1975 constitutional crisis",
  "On 11 November 1975 the Governor-General removed an elected Prime Minister from office. Half a century later, Australians still argue about whether it was a constitutional necessity or a step too far.",
  [
    ["11 November 1975", "On 11 November 1975 the Governor-General dismissed Prime Minister Gough Whitlam, the only time this has happened federally.",
      "Just after one o'clock on 11 November 1975, Gough Whitlam arrived at Government House in Canberra expecting a routine meeting. Governor-General Sir John Kerr instead handed him a letter terminating his commission. Within hours, Kerr had appointed Opposition Leader Malcolm Fraser as caretaker Prime Minister. That afternoon, on the steps of Parliament House, Kerr's official secretary read the proclamation dissolving both houses. Whitlam, standing behind him, told the crowd: 'Well may we say God save the Queen, because nothing will save the Governor-General.' It is the only time a Governor-General has dismissed a federal Prime Minister. To see why, go back several weeks to a fight over money."],
    ["A government without supply", "The crisis began when the Opposition-controlled Senate deferred the budget bills, helped by two non-Labor Senate appointments.",
      "Governments need Parliament to approve spending through appropriation bills, called supply. Without it, money for public servants and services eventually runs out. Whitlam's Labor government, elected in 1972 and re-elected in 1974, controlled the House but not the Senate. In 1975 it faced controversies, the worst being the Loans Affair, in which ministers sought a very large overseas loan through unconventional channels. In October the Coalition, led by Fraser, used its Senate numbers to defer the supply bills until Whitlam called an election. He refused, arguing a government's survival should depend on the House, not the Senate.\n\nThose numbers were themselves controversial. Two Labor Senate vacancies, in New South Wales and Queensland, were filled by non-Labor appointees, breaking the usual convention. The states acted within their legal powers."],
    ["Kerr's decision", "Kerr used the Crown's reserve powers, concluding that a government without supply must go to an election.",
      "Section 64 of the Constitution says ministers hold office during the Governor-General's pleasure, and section 61 gives the Governor-General executive power. Normally these are used only on the Prime Minister's advice. But the Governor-General also has reserve powers that can be used in rare cases without advice. Kerr concluded that a government unable to obtain supply should either advise an election or be dismissed. He sought the view of Chief Justice Sir Garfield Barwick, who advised in writing that dismissal was within his power.\n\nKerr did not warn Whitlam. He later said he feared Whitlam would ask the Queen to remove him first. Fraser was commissioned on condition he obtain supply and advise a double dissolution, which happened that same afternoon."],
    ["The people decide", "Voters gave the Coalition a landslide in December 1975, yet serious scholars still disagree about the dismissal.",
      "The dismissal unleashed fierce emotion. Protesters gathered outside Parliament House, and Labor's slogan became a call to maintain the rage. The House passed a no-confidence motion in Fraser, but Parliament was dissolved before the Speaker could tell the Governor-General. At the 13 December 1975 election, the Coalition won a large majority in both houses. Supporters called it the people's endorsement. Critics said victory could not prove the method was proper.\n\nSupporters argue the Senate may reject money bills, a government that cannot pay its bills cannot govern, and the voters should decide. Critics argue the Senate should not bring down a government the House backs, Kerr should have warned Whitlam, and an unelected official should not remove an elected Prime Minister. Some also question whether Barwick should have advised at all."],
    ["The legacy", "The crisis led to a constitutional change, a likely new convention, and later revelations from the Palace Letters.",
      "In 1977 voters approved a referendum requiring a replacement senator to come from the same party as the senator who left, closing the loophole exposed in 1975. No Opposition since has blocked supply in the same way, and many observers think a new convention has formed against it. The episode also fed later debates about whether Australia should become a republic.\n\nIn 2020, after a High Court case brought by historian Jenny Hocking, the National Archives released Kerr's correspondence with the Queen's private secretary, known as the Palace Letters. They showed Kerr had discussed the reserve powers with the Palace beforehand, but did not tell the Queen in advance of his decision."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What made 11 November 1975 unusual?", ["Parliament voted to dissolve itself in protest","The Queen personally removed the Prime Minister","A Governor-General dismissed an elected Prime Minister","The Prime Minister resigned during a national emergency"], 2, "Kerr's dismissal of Whitlam is the only time a Governor-General has removed a federal Prime Minister."],
    [1, "What triggered the 1975 crisis?", ["The Senate deferring the government's supply bills","A High Court ruling against the government","A failed referendum on the Senate","The Prime Minister's resignation"], 0, "Without supply, the government's money would eventually run out."],
    [2, "What did Kerr conclude about a government that cannot obtain supply?", ["It could keep governing until the money ran out","It should wait for the High Court to decide","It should ask the Queen to settle the dispute","It should either advise an election or be dismissed"], 3, "Kerr used the reserve powers on that reasoning, after Barwick advised he had the power."],
    [3, "What is a main argument made by critics of the dismissal?", ["The Constitution gives the Senate no power over money bills","An unelected official should not remove an elected leader","The Constitution forbids any election during a crisis","The supply bills had already been passed by both houses"], 1, "Critics say the decision let an unelected Governor-General override an elected government."],
    [4, "What did the 1977 referendum change?", ["It made Australia a republic with an elected president","It abolished the Governor-General's reserve powers","It made replacement senators come from the same party","It ended the Senate's power to block money bills"], 2, "This closed the casual vacancy loophole exposed in 1975."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"He expected a routine meeting. Hours later, Australia had a new Prime Minister."});

WB.L("mabo-native-title", "society", "intermediate", "Mabo and the end of terra nullius",
  "In 1992 a man from a tiny island in the Torres Strait changed the foundations of Australian land law. The Mabo decision overturned a legal fiction that had stood since 1788 and reshaped the national conversation about who owns the country.",
  [
    ["A garden on Mer", "Eddie Mabo discovered that Australian law gave his family's island land to the Crown, on the fiction that the continent had no owners.",
      "Mer, or Murray Island, is a small volcanic island in the eastern Torres Strait. For generations the Meriam people have tended gardens there, with plots passed down through families under their own laws. Eddie Koiki Mabo grew up on Mer and later worked on the mainland, including as a gardener at James Cook University in Townsville. In the 1970s academics there told him that under Australian law his family's land belonged to the Crown. To Mabo this made no sense.\n\nThe law rested on a fiction often called terra nullius, Latin for land belonging to no one: that the continent in 1788 had no recognisable system of ownership. A 1971 case, Milirrpum v Nabalco, brought by Yolngu people, accepted they had a sophisticated system of law but ruled it gave no property rights recognised by Australian law."],
    ["A ten-year fight", "The Mabo case took a decade and included an earlier High Court win over Queensland.",
      "In 1982 Eddie Mabo and four other Meriam people, including David Passi and James Rice, began a High Court case claiming their traditional land rights on Mer. In 1985 the Queensland Government passed a law declaring any such rights had been wiped out when the islands were annexed in 1879. In 1988, in Mabo No 1, the High Court struck that law down because it was inconsistent with the federal Racial Discrimination Act. The main case then returned to fact-finding, with weeks of evidence about Meriam customs and land holding. It was gruelling, expensive and slow. Eddie Mabo died of cancer in January 1992, months before the final decision."],
    ["The decision", "On 3 June 1992 the High Court recognised native title by six judges to one, but with limits.",
      "On 3 June 1992, in Mabo v Queensland No 2, the High Court ruled six judges to one that the Meriam people held native title to most of the land on Mer. More broadly, it held that Australia's common law recognises native title, reflecting the rights of Indigenous peoples under their traditional laws and customs. The judges rejected the idea that the land belonged to no one as a basis for denying those rights, calling it out of step with history and modern values.\n\nBut the decision set limits. Valid government acts, such as granting freehold, can extinguish native title, and it survives only where a group has kept up its connection with the land."],
    ["From ruling to law", "Parliament created a claims system in 1993, and native title varies by place and usually sits alongside other people's rights.",
      "The decision raised urgent questions. After negotiating with Indigenous leaders, industry and the states, the Keating government passed the Native Title Act 1993. It confirmed existing titles, set up a claims process and created the National Native Title Tribunal; the Federal Court decides or approves claims. After the 1996 Wik decision found pastoral leases did not necessarily extinguish native title, the Howard government passed major amendments in 1998.\n\nNative title does not threaten suburban backyards, because freehold generally extinguishes it. What it includes depends on a group's traditional laws and the rights already over the land. Sometimes it means exclusive possession. More often it is shared rights to camp, hunt, fish, hold ceremonies or be consulted, alongside pastoralists, miners or the public. Proving a claim can be very hard for peoples forcibly moved off their country."],
    ["A lasting legacy", "Mabo changed Australian law and remains a landmark in debates about history and reconciliation.",
      "Reactions were sharply divided in the 1990s. Some business groups and politicians warned of uncertainty for mining and farming, while many Indigenous advocates felt the system that followed gave them too little. Over time, native title has been recognised over a large share of the continent, often through negotiated agreements rather than courtroom battles, though the rights involved vary greatly.\n\nThe anniversary of the judgment, 3 June, is now marked as Mabo Day. It closes National Reconciliation Week, which begins on 27 May, the date of the 1967 referendum. Eddie Mabo did not live to see his victory, but his name marks the moment Australian law formally recognised that the land had owners long before 1788."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Mabo's case challenge?", ["The idea that Queensland could not make its own laws","The idea that the High Court could hear land claims","The idea that the continent had no owners in 1788","The idea that freehold land could ever be sold"], 2, "The terra nullius fiction treated the land as belonging to no one, so Indigenous peoples had no legal title."],
    [1, "Why did the High Court strike down Queensland's 1985 law in 1988?", ["It clashed with the Racial Discrimination Act","It had not received the Governor's assent","It breached the constitutional right to vote","It applied only to land used for mining"], 0, "The 1988 decision cleared the way for the main case to continue."],
    [2, "What did the 1992 decision conclude about native title?", ["Australian courts can never recognise it at all","It automatically overrides all freehold owners","It applies only on islands of the Torres Strait","It exists, but freehold grants can extinguish it"], 3, "The court recognised native title but accepted it can be extinguished by valid government acts."],
    [3, "What generally happens to native title on freehold land?", ["It overrides the freehold owner","It is generally extinguished","It is shared equally","It becomes a lease"], 1, "Freehold grants generally extinguish native title, so it does not threaten suburban backyards."],
    [4, "Why is Mabo remembered as a turning point?", ["It abolished all pastoral leases","It gave Indigenous people ownership of the whole continent","It formally recognised that the land had owners before 1788","It made Queensland a federal territory"], 2, "The decision overturned the idea that the land belonged to no one."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A gardener's question about his family's land changed Australian law."});

WB.L("nuremberg-trials", "society", "advanced", "The Nuremberg trials and the birth of international criminal law",
  "In 1945 the leaders of a defeated regime were put on trial before the world for crimes so vast there were barely words for them. The Nuremberg trials invented much of modern international law, and their critics raised questions we are still answering.",
  [
    ["A trial in the ruins", "In 1945 the Allies chose to put surviving Nazi leaders on trial in Nuremberg rather than simply execute them.",
      "On 20 November 1945, twenty-one men sat in a wooden dock in a Nuremberg courtroom, among them Hermann Göring, once Hitler's designated successor, and Rudolf Hess, the former deputy leader. Much of the city lay in ruins. Its Palace of Justice had survived with a prison attached, and Nuremberg had hosted the Nazi Party's huge rallies. Interpreters translated proceedings simultaneously into four languages, a novelty then.\n\nSome Allied leaders, at times including Winston Churchill, favoured simply shooting top Nazis. Others, especially in the United States, argued a trial would test the evidence, let the accused defend themselves and build a public record. In August 1945 the United States, Britain, France and the Soviet Union signed the London Charter, creating the International Military Tribunal."],
    ["New crimes, new words", "The tribunal judged conspiracy, crimes against peace, war crimes and crimes against humanity.",
      "The London Charter set out the charges. Crimes against peace covered planning and waging wars of aggression. War crimes covered breaches of the laws of war, such as murdering prisoners or civilians in occupied territory. Crimes against humanity covered murder, extermination, enslavement, deportation and persecution of civilians. A count of conspiracy tied these together.\n\nCrimes against humanity were especially important, because they reached acts a government committed against its own people, which traditional international law had largely ignored. A related word was also coming into use. In 1944 the Polish-Jewish lawyer Raphael Lemkin had coined 'genocide' for the deliberate destruction of a people. It was mentioned at Nuremberg, and in 1948 the United Nations adopted a convention against it."],
    ["Evidence and verdicts", "Most defendants were convicted, three were acquitted, and following orders was no excuse.",
      "The prosecution relied heavily on the Nazis' own documents, including orders, minutes and reports, and on film shot by Allied troops in liberated concentration camps. Survivors and former officials testified. The trial lasted almost a year. On 1 October 1946, twelve defendants were sentenced to death, including Martin Bormann, tried in his absence. Seven received prison terms, from ten years to life. Three were acquitted, which showed the judges were not simply backing the prosecution.\n\nThe tribunal rejected the defence of superior orders: following orders could reduce a sentence but did not excuse a crime. Göring took his own life the night before the executions, which were carried out on 16 October 1946."],
    ["The case against Nuremberg", "Critics argued the trials were victors' justice and applied some laws retrospectively.",
      "Even at the time, serious people had doubts. The most common criticism was victors' justice: only the defeated were tried, while Allied actions such as the area bombing of German cities were never examined. The Soviet Union's presence was awkward, since it had invaded Poland in 1939 alongside Germany, and its prosecutors tried to blame the Katyn massacre of Polish officers on the Germans, though the Soviets were responsible. The tribunal did not convict on that charge. Others argued crimes against peace were not clearly crimes when committed, breaching the principle that no one should be punished under a law made after the fact. Defenders replied that aggression had already been outlawed by treaty and that the atrocities were crimes under any civilised legal system."],
    ["From Nuremberg to The Hague", "The trials established that individuals can answer for state crimes and led to the International Criminal Court, though enforcement remains uneven.",
      "Other trials followed. The Tokyo tribunal tried Japanese leaders, with Australian judge Sir William Webb as president. American courts at Nuremberg held twelve further trials, and the doctors' trial produced the Nuremberg Code on ethical medical research. The Cold War then froze the project until the 1990s, when the United Nations created tribunals for the former Yugoslavia and Rwanda. Countries adopted the Rome Statute in 1998, and the permanent International Criminal Court began work in The Hague in 2002. Australia ratified the Statute that year.\n\nNuremberg's lasting principle is that individuals, even heads of state, are personally responsible for the gravest crimes. Enforcement remains hard: courts depend on states to arrest suspects, and the United States, China and Russia are not ICC members."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did some Allied leaders favour a trial over summary executions?", ["It would be cheaper and quicker than executions","It would test the evidence and leave a public record","Allied treaties required trials for all prisoners","German law required its leaders to be tried"], 1, "A courtroom could test evidence, let the accused defend themselves and create a record that could not easily be denied."],
    [1, "What made crimes against humanity especially significant?", ["It applied only to soldiers on the battlefield","It replaced the existing laws of war entirely","It covered only economic crimes such as looting","It covered a government's acts against its own people"], 3, "Traditional international law had largely ignored how states treated their own citizens."],
    [2, "How did the tribunal treat the defence of following superior orders?", ["It was no excuse, but could lessen a sentence","It was accepted as a complete defence","It was accepted only for low-ranking staff","It was ruled irrelevant even to sentencing"], 0, "Following orders could mitigate punishment but did not remove guilt."],
    [3, "What is the criticism known as victors' justice?", ["All of the judges were German citizens","The trials were held entirely in secret","Only the losing side was put on trial","Every defendant was found not guilty"], 2, "Allied conduct, such as area bombing, was never examined."],
    [4, "What is Nuremberg's lasting principle?", ["Only states, never individuals, can be put on trial","Even heads of state can be held personally responsible","Following orders removes all personal responsibility","Courts can arrest suspects without any state help"], 1, "Acting for a government is no shield, though enforcement still depends on states."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"The Nuremberg judges acquitted three men. What that said about the trial."});

WB.L("votes-for-women-au", "society", "beginner", "Votes for women: how Australia led the world",
  "In the 1890s, a colony on the edge of the British Empire did something almost no government on Earth had done: it let women vote and stand for parliament. The story of how Australian women won the vote is full of determined campaigners, giant petitions and some uncomfortable exceptions.",
  [
    ["A petition 260 metres long", "Campaigners won attention with enormous petitions, public meetings and relentless lobbying.",
      "In 1891 Victorian campaigners presented parliament with a petition so long it was rolled up like a carpet. The Monster Petition carried around 30,000 signatures, gathered door to door by women across the colony. It did not succeed straight away, but it showed that huge numbers of ordinary women wanted a say. Similar petitions spread across the colonies in the 1880s and 1890s. Campaigners held public meetings, wrote to newspapers and lobbied members of parliament one by one. Many also belonged to the Woman's Christian Temperance Union, which campaigned against alcohol abuse and saw the vote as a way for women to protect their families and communities."],
    ["South Australia goes first", "In 1894 South Australia became the first place in the world to let women both vote and stand for parliament.",
      "New Zealand gave women the vote in national elections in 1893, the first self-governing country to do so. South Australia followed in December 1894 and went further: women could both vote and stand for parliament, a world first. Mary Lee, an Irish-born widow, had campaigned for years, and a petition of more than 11,000 signatures reached parliament in 1894. Opponents hoped adding the right to stand would make the bill too radical to pass. It backfired. South Australian women first voted in 1896.\n\nOpponents said politics was too rough for women, or that wives would copy their husbands. Supporters said women paid taxes and obeyed the laws, so deserved a say."],
    ["A nation with a catch", "The Commonwealth Franchise Act 1902 gave most women the federal vote but excluded most Aboriginal people.",
      "When the colonies federated in 1901, the new Parliament had to decide who could vote. The Commonwealth Franchise Act 1902 gave women the right to vote and stand for federal Parliament on the same terms as men, making Australia one of the first nations to do so. But the Act also excluded most Aboriginal people, and people from Asia, Africa and the Pacific Islands other than New Zealand, unless they were already entitled to vote in their state. In practice, many Aboriginal people were denied the vote for decades. They gained the clear right to enrol for federal elections in 1962, and enrolment and voting became compulsory for them, as for everyone else, in 1984."],
    ["First candidates, slow progress", "Women could stand long before they were elected in numbers, and the first federal women arrived only in 1943.",
      "Catherine Helen Spence, a South Australian writer and reformer, made history in 1897 by standing for the convention that drafted the Constitution, Australia's first female political candidate. At the 1903 federal election, four women stood, the first in the British Empire to contest a national parliament. The best known, Vida Goldstein, ran for the Senate in Victoria and won tens of thousands of votes but was not elected. The others were Selina Anderson, Nellie Martel and Mary Moore-Bentley.\n\nWestern Australia gave women the vote in 1899, and Victoria was the last state, in 1908. Edith Cowan became the first woman elected to an Australian parliament in 1921, in Western Australia. Federally it took until 1943: Enid Lyons (United Australia Party) and Dorothy Tangney (Labor). Women remained a small minority for decades."],
    ["Pride and its limits", "Australia's early start is a point of pride, and a reminder that rights are often won only in part.",
      "Australia's suffrage story is often told as a simple triumph, and there is real reason for pride. A young democracy on the far side of the world helped lead a global change that took many countries, including Britain and the United States, decades longer. Yet the same laws that welcomed many women into political life shut out most Aboriginal women and men. Rights are rarely won all at once.\n\nToday voting is compulsory for adult citizens, and you can check your enrolment on the Australian Electoral Commission's website. Every ballot you cast uses a right people campaigned for, petition by petition, for decades."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the Monster Petition show politicians?", ["That most women opposed voting","That only wealthy women cared about politics","That huge numbers of ordinary women wanted a say","That petitions were illegal in Victoria"], 2, "Around 30,000 signatures, gathered door to door, could not be ignored."],
    [1, "What did South Australia's 1894 law achieve that was a world first?", ["Compulsory voting for all adults","Votes at 18 for women and men","Paid wages for members of parliament","Letting women vote and stand for election"], 3, "New Zealand gave women the vote in 1893, but South Australia also let them stand."],
    [2, "Who was largely excluded by the Commonwealth Franchise Act 1902?", ["Most Aboriginal people","Women over 30","Landowners","People born in Britain"], 0, "Most Aboriginal people were denied the federal vote unless already enrolled in their state."],
    [3, "How quickly did women win seats once they could stand?", ["Women won many seats straight after 1902","It took four decades to elect a woman federally","No woman has ever won a seat federally","Women held half of parliament by 1910"], 1, "Winning the vote came long before women were elected in significant numbers."],
    [4, "Why is the suffrage story not a simple triumph?", ["Women were not allowed to vote federally until 1962","Voting was banned in most colonies","The same laws shut out most Aboriginal women and men","Only men could stand for parliament until 1943"], 2, "Rights are rarely won all at once, and the 1902 Act excluded most Aboriginal people."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A petition so long it was rolled like a carpet. How Australian women won the vote."});

WB.L("social-contract", "society", "advanced", "The social contract: why we agree to be governed",
  "Why should anyone obey the government? For nearly four centuries, philosophers have answered with a powerful thought experiment: imagine life with no rulers at all, then ask what deal reasonable people would strike.",
  [
    ["Hobbes: war of all", "Hobbes argued that without a powerful ruler, life would be a constant war of fear and conflict.",
      "Picture waking to find no police, courts, laws or government. Nothing would stop a stronger neighbour taking your food or home. This imagined situation is the 'state of nature', the starting point of the social contract: the idea that governments are legitimate because the governed, in some sense, agree to them, trading some freedom for security and order.\n\nThomas Hobbes lived through the English Civil War. In Leviathan (1651) he argued that people in a state of nature would be roughly equal in their ability to harm one another, and constantly suspicious. The result: a war of every person against every person, with no farming, industry or arts, and life 'solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short'. The only escape was a single sovereign strong enough to keep the peace. Even a harsh ruler beat chaos."],
    ["Locke and natural rights", "Locke argued that people have rights before government exists, and governments that violate them lose legitimacy.",
      "John Locke painted a gentler picture. In Two Treatises of Government (around 1689) he argued that even in a state of nature people are bound by a natural law forbidding harm to others' life, health, liberty or possessions. People form governments not to escape chaos but to protect these rights more reliably, with impartial judges and enforcement.\n\nThat changes everything. A government built to protect rights can lose its legitimacy by violating them, and people may then resist and replace it. His ideas about consent and natural rights echoed in the American Declaration of Independence in 1776, which speaks of unalienable rights to life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness."],
    ["Rousseau and the general will", "Rousseau argued that a legitimate society makes its own laws according to the shared common interest.",
      "Geneva-born Jean-Jacques Rousseau took the idea in another direction. His book The Social Contract (1762) opens with the line usually translated as 'Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains.' He thought existing societies had corrupted people and entrenched inequality. A legitimate society, he argued, is one where citizens collectively make their own laws, guided by the general will: what is genuinely in the common interest, not just the sum of private wants. In obeying such laws, people would in effect obey themselves. His ideas inspired democratic and revolutionary movements, especially in France. Critics worried the general will could be twisted to justify forcing people to conform."],
    ["But did anyone sign?", "Critics point out that no real person ever agreed to the social contract.",
      "The obvious objection is that no one ever signed anything. You were born into a country with laws already in place. In the 18th century the Scottish philosopher David Hume argued that most governments began in conquest or usurpation, not agreement. Some theorists replied that by staying and accepting a country's benefits, you give tacit consent. Hume answered that this is like saying a man carried aboard a ship while asleep consents to the captain because he could leap into the ocean.\n\nLater critics, including some feminist scholars, argued that historical versions of the contract quietly excluded women and colonised peoples from the agreement altogether."],
    ["Rawls and the veil", "Rawls revived the social contract as a test of fairness, and its questions still run through modern political debate.",
      "In 1971 the American philosopher John Rawls revived the idea in A Theory of Justice, not as history but as a test of fairness. Imagine designing a society from behind a veil of ignorance, not knowing whether you would be rich or poor, talented or not, or which gender, race or religion you would have. Rawls argued you would choose equal basic liberties and accept inequality only if it benefits the least advantaged. In 1974 his Harvard colleague Robert Nozick countered that people's rights to what they have earned strictly limit what a state may redistribute, defending a much smaller government.\n\nThese debates echo in arguments over taxes, welfare and trading freedom for safety. When someone claims authority over you, ask whether the people affected could genuinely agree to it."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Hobbes think people should accept a powerful ruler?", ["Rulers always protect natural rights","It prevents constant fear and conflict","Citizens could then vote on every law","It guarantees equal wealth for everyone"], 1, "Hobbes thought only a powerful sovereign could prevent a war of all against all."],
    [1, "According to Locke, what can people do if a government violates their natural rights?", ["Nothing, since all rule is legitimate","Only petition the monarch","Resist and replace it","Leave the country"], 2, "Locke tied legitimacy to protecting rights, so violating them could forfeit it."],
    [2, "What did Rousseau mean by the general will?", ["What is in the common interest, not private wants","Whatever a majority wants at any given moment","The decisions of one wise and fair king","The combined wishes of the wealthiest citizens"], 0, "For Rousseau, obeying laws made in the common interest means citizens obey themselves."],
    [3, "What point did Hume make with his ship comparison?", ["Strong captains are needed to run a ship","Citizens should be free to emigrate at will","Governments are best run like businesses","Staying is not consent if leaving is unrealistic"], 3, "Hume doubted that tacit consent showed genuine agreement."],
    [4, "What is Rawls's veil of ignorance designed to test?", ["How much ordinary people know about politics","Which rules people pick not knowing their place","Whether voters are well informed before elections","How secret ballots keep each vote private"], 1, "Not knowing your position pushes you to choose principles that are fair to everyone."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Imagine no government at all. Philosophers use that to ask why we obey."});

